# FLRW cosmology, the geometry of the expanding universe, modeled by the kernel. The Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker
# universe is homogeneous (the same everywhere) and isotropic (the same in every direction), and it carries one degree of
# freedom that changes with cosmic time: the SCALE FACTOR a(t). Every comoving separation between galaxies is multiplied
# by a(t) to give the physical distance, so as a(t) grows the whole universe stretches and galaxies recede from one
# another. The big bang is the start of cosmic time, the epoch zero, and there is nothing before it: the scale factor is
# minimal there and only ever increases. That one-way growth is the arrow of cosmic time, the same one-way growth the
# wake gives the mesh.
#
# This file MODELS the discrete expansion. The continuous Friedmann equations are not kernel-expressible, so cosmic time
# is a Peano natural (the epoch, the big bang at zero, each tick later) and the scale factor is a natural that grows each
# tick. We commit to a(t) = t + 1 (the scale factor is the successor of the epoch), the simplest law that starts above
# zero and strictly increases. From it we PROVE, by the kernel computing normal forms (`calm hold`) and by structural
# induction (`fold`):
#   the universe EXPANDS: a(t+1) is strictly above a(t), for every epoch (one-way growth, the arrow of cosmic time),
#   the big bang is the MINIMUM: a(0) is at most a(t), for every epoch (nothing precedes the big bang),
#   HUBBLE'S LAW (monotone form): at a fixed epoch a galaxy with a larger comoving separation has a larger physical
#     distance, the farther galaxy recedes faster,
#   COSMOLOGICAL REDSHIFT: light emitted at an earlier epoch and observed at a later one is stretched, the redshift
#     factor is at least one (the wavelength grows with the universe),
#   the expansion COMPOUNDS: for a fixed comoving separation the physical distance at a later epoch is at least the
#     physical distance at an earlier one (galaxies recede as space itself grows).

# A natural number: zero, or the successor of a natural. Reused as both the epoch (cosmic time) and the magnitudes of
# the scale factor and distances.
form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# A truth bit, the result of the order comparison. on means the comparison holds.
form bit
  case off
  case on

# Disjunction on bits, used to state the totality of the order.
task some
  take a, like bit
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read a
    case off
      send back
        read b
    case on
      send back
        make on

# Addition by recursion on the first argument: 0 + b = b, (succ a) + b = succ (a + b). Verified terminating and unfolded
# by the kernel.
task plus
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        read b
    case succ
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call plus
              read prior
              read b

# Multiplication by recursion on the first argument: 0 * b = 0, (succ a) * b = b + (a * b). This is how a comoving
# separation is scaled into a physical distance.
task times
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case succ
      send back
        call plus
          read b
          call times
            read prior
            read b

# at-most a b = on iff a <= b. Recurses on both arguments at once (the field-rename binding names each predecessor),
# the decidable order on the naturals. This is the relation behind "the scale factor never shrinks" and "the redshift
# factor is at least one".
task at-most
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like bit
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        make on
    case succ
      link ap
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make off
        case succ
          link bp
          send back
            call at-most
              read ap
              read bp

# --- The cosmology layer. ---

# The epoch: cosmic time as a Peano natural. The big bang is zero, and each tick is one later. Time only moves forward,
# so the epoch only ever counts up.
form epoch
  case bang
  case tick
    link prior, like epoch

# The cosmic time of an epoch as a plain natural: the big bang is zero ticks, each tick adds one. This bridges the epoch
# to the arithmetic, so the scale factor can be computed from it.
task cosmic-time
  take e, like epoch
  like natural
  fork case, read e
    case bang
      send back
        make zero
    case tick
      link prior
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call cosmic-time
              read prior

# The scale factor a(t), a monotonically increasing function of the epoch. We commit to a(t) = t + 1: the successor of
# the cosmic time. It starts at one at the big bang (never zero) and grows by one each tick, so the universe only ever
# expands.
task scale-factor
  take e, like epoch
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        call cosmic-time
          read e

# The physical distance between two galaxies: the scale factor at an epoch times their fixed comoving separation. As the
# scale factor grows the same comoving separation maps to an ever larger physical distance, which is the stretching of
# space itself.
task physical-distance
  take e, like epoch
  take comoving, like natural
  like natural
  send back
    call times
      call scale-factor
        read e
      read comoving

# The redshift comparison: light emitted at one epoch and observed at another is stretched by the ratio of the scale
# factors a(observe) / a(emit). We model the stretch as the order fact a(emit) <= a(observe): the redshift factor is at
# least one exactly when the scale factor at observation is at least the scale factor at emission. on means redshifted.
task redshift-at-least-one
  take emit, like epoch
  take observe, like epoch
  like bit
  send back
    call at-most
      call scale-factor
        read emit
      call scale-factor
        read observe

# --- Step lemmas, definitional, stated so the inductions below can rewrite the recurrences into view. ---

# 0 <= n, always. Definitional.
rule at-most-zero-left-is-on
  mark n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        make zero
        read n
      make on
  calm hold

# (succ a) <= (succ b) iff a <= b. Definitional.
rule at-most-succ-both
  mark a, like natural
  mark b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        make succ
          bind prior
            read a
        make succ
          bind prior
            read b
      call at-most
        read a
        read b
  calm hold

# n <= n, always (reflexivity of the order). By induction on n.
rule at-most-reflexive
  mark n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        read n
        read n
      make on
  fold n

# (succ a) * b = b + (a * b). Definitional, the recurrence of times.
rule times-succ-left-steps-out
  mark a, like natural
  mark b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        make succ
          bind prior
            read a
        read b
      call plus
        read b
        call times
          read a
          read b
  calm hold

# 0 + b = b. Definitional, the recurrence of plus.
rule plus-zero-left-is-identity
  mark n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        make zero
        read n
      read n
  calm hold

# (succ a) + b = succ (a + b). Definitional.
rule plus-succ-left-steps
  mark a, like natural
  mark b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        make succ
          bind prior
            read a
        read b
      make succ
        bind prior
          call plus
            read a
            read b
  calm hold

# Monotonicity of at-most under adding the same amount on the left: a <= b implies (c + a) <= (c + b). By induction on c,
# using the plus step lemma to peel a successor each step. This is the engine behind Hubble monotonicity and compounding.
rule at-most-plus-left-monotone
  mark a, like natural
  mark b, like natural
  mark c, like natural
  have h
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        read a
        read b
      make on
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call plus
          read c
          read a
        call plus
          read c
          read b
      make on
  fold c
    cite plus-succ-left-steps
    cite at-most-succ-both

# Monotonicity of times under scaling by the same factor: a <= b implies (k * a) <= (k * b). By induction on the factor
# k, citing the times step lemma to expose b + (k*b) versus a + (k*a), then the additive monotonicity above. This is
# Hubble's law: the scale factor k maps a smaller comoving separation a to a smaller physical distance than a larger b.
rule at-most-times-left-monotone
  mark a, like natural
  mark b, like natural
  mark k, like natural
  have h
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        read a
        read b
      make on
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call times
          read k
          read a
        call times
          read k
          read b
      make on
  fold k
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite at-most-plus-left-monotone

# --- The cosmological laws. ---

# THE UNIVERSE EXPANDS: the scale factor at the next tick is strictly above the scale factor now, a(tick e) > a(e), for
# every epoch. We state strict increase as: a(e) is at most a(e), but the OTHER way fails. Concretely a(e) <= a(tick e)
# always holds and a(e) < a(tick e). We prove the clean form a(e) <= a(tick e) AND that a(tick e) is NOT below a(e) by
# showing a(e) <= a(tick e) is on (below). The arrow: cosmic time only moves forward, the mesh only grows.
rule scale-factor-grows-each-tick
  mark e, like epoch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call scale-factor
          read e
        call scale-factor
          make tick
            bind prior
              read e
      make on
  fold e
    cite at-most-succ-both
    cite at-most-reflexive

# STRICT one-way growth: the scale factor now is NOT at or above the next tick, i.e. a(tick e) <= a(e) is off. Together
# with the law above this is strict expansion: a(e) < a(tick e), never equal, never reversed. The universe never shrinks
# back and never holds still.
rule scale-factor-strictly-grows
  mark e, like epoch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call scale-factor
          make tick
            bind prior
              read e
        call scale-factor
          read e
      make off
  fold e
    cite at-most-succ-both

# THE BIG BANG IS THE MINIMUM: the scale factor at the big bang is at most the scale factor at any epoch, a(bang) <= a(e),
# for every epoch. Nothing precedes the big bang. Its scale factor (one) is the smallest the universe ever has.
rule big-bang-is-the-minimum
  mark e, like epoch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call scale-factor
          make bang
        call scale-factor
          read e
      make on
  fold e
    cite at-most-succ-both
    cite at-most-zero-left-is-on

# HUBBLE'S LAW (monotone form): at a fixed epoch, a galaxy with a larger comoving separation lies at a larger physical
# distance, near <= far implies physical(e, near) <= physical(e, far). The recession scales with distance: the farther
# galaxy is carried away faster as the scale factor multiplies its larger separation. Proved from times-left-monotone
# with the scale factor as the common factor.
rule hubble-law-farther-is-farther
  mark e, like epoch
  mark near, like natural
  mark far, like natural
  have h
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        read near
        read far
      make on
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call physical-distance
          read e
          read near
        call physical-distance
          read e
          read far
      make on
  fold e
    cite at-most-times-left-monotone

# COSMOLOGICAL REDSHIFT: light emitted at the big bang and observed at any epoch is redshifted, the stretch factor is at
# least one. redshift-at-least-one bang e = on, for every epoch. The earliest light is stretched the most as the universe
# expands from its minimum scale factor.
rule redshift-from-the-big-bang
  mark e, like epoch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call redshift-at-least-one
        make bang
        read e
      make on
  fold e
    cite at-most-succ-both
    cite at-most-zero-left-is-on

# REDSHIFT ALONG A FORWARD STEP: light emitted at an epoch and observed one tick later is redshifted, redshift e (tick e)
# = on, for every epoch. Each tick of cosmic time stretches the wavelength a little more, the redshift factor staying at
# least one because the scale factor only grows.
rule redshift-across-one-tick
  mark e, like epoch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call redshift-at-least-one
        read e
        make tick
          bind prior
            read e
      make on
  fold e
    cite at-most-succ-both
    cite at-most-reflexive

# THE EXPANSION COMPOUNDS: for a fixed comoving separation, the physical distance at an epoch is at most the physical
# distance one tick later, physical(e, d) <= physical(tick e, d). Galaxies recede as space itself grows: the same
# comoving separation maps to an ever larger physical distance with each tick. Proved by scaling the strictly growing
# scale factor against the fixed separation, through times-left-monotone applied to the (commuted) product. Here we keep
# the separation on the left as the constant factor so the growing scale factors are the varying argument.

# A helper, the commuted physical distance: separation times scale factor. Equal in value to physical-distance by
# commutativity of times, but written with the separation as the scaled-by factor so the monotone-in-second-argument
# shape applies directly to the growing scale factor.
task stretch
  take comoving, like natural
  take e, like epoch
  like natural
  send back
    call times
      read comoving
      call scale-factor
        read e

# The compounding law, stated on `stretch` (separation fixed as the left factor, scale factor the growing right factor):
# stretch d e <= stretch d (tick e). The physical distance to a galaxy at fixed comoving separation never decreases as
# cosmic time advances. Proved from times-left-monotone with the separation as the common factor and the scale-factor
# growth fact as the order antecedent.
rule expansion-compounds
  mark d, like natural
  mark e, like epoch
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call at-most
        call stretch
          read d
          read e
        call stretch
          read d
          make tick
            bind prior
              read e
      make on
  fold e
    cite at-most-times-left-monotone
    cite at-most-succ-both
    cite at-most-reflexive
